{"name":"Salt Island Green","hex":"#757261","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"salt","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A common substance, chemically consisting mainly of sodium chloride (NaCl), used extensively as a food ingredient, seasoning, condiment, and preservative.","One of the compounds formed from the reaction of an acid with a base, where a positive ion replaces a hydrogen of the acid."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of water: containing salt, saline.","Treated with salt as a preservative; cured with salt, salted."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/salt"},{"term":"island","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, totally surrounded by water.","A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, partially surrounded by water; a peninsula; a half-island."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To surround with water; make into an island.","To set, dot (as if) with islands."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/island"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Island Green","blocks":[{"text":"Island Green is a historic brewing site in Wrexham city centre, North Wales, home to the former site of the Island Green Brewery. Following the closure of the brewery in 1931, and the abandonment of the site in the 1970s, the area was converted into a retail park in 1999, containing Wrexham Central railway station, as well as residences which utilised the former brewery buildings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Island_Green_retail_park%2C_Wrexham.JPG/330px-Island_Green_retail_park%2C_Wrexham.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island_Green"},{"title":"Salt","blocks":[{"text":"Salt is a mineral composed primarily of sodium chloride (NaCl). When used in food, especially in granulated form, it is more formally called table salt. In the form of a natural crystalline mineral, salt is also known as rock salt or halite. Salt is essential for life in general, and saltiness is one of the basic human tastes. Salt is one of the oldest and most ubiquitous food seasonings, and is known to uniformly improve the taste perception of food. Salting, brining, and pickling are ancient and important methods of food preservation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg/330px-Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt"},{"title":"Island","blocks":[{"text":"An island or isle is a piece of land, distinct from a continent, completely surrounded by water. There are continental islands, which were formed by being split from a continent by plate tectonics, and oceanic islands, which have never been part of a continent. Oceanic islands can be formed from volcanic activity, grow into atolls from coral reefs, and form from sediment along shorelines, creating barrier islands. River islands can also form from sediment and debris in rivers. Artificial islands are those made by humans, including small rocky outcroppings built out of lagoons and large-scale land reclamation projects used for development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg/330px-Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/The_rear_of_the_Island_Green_Brewery_building%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4786544.jpg/330px-The_rear_of_the_Island_Green_Brewery_building%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4786544.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/The_rear_of_the_Island_Green_Brewery_building%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4786544.jpg/330px-The_rear_of_the_Island_Green_Brewery_building%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4786544.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rear of the former brewery buildings, with the River Gwenfro to the left.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_rear_of_the_Island_Green_Brewery_building%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4786544.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Island_Green_Shopping_Centre%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_605649.jpg/500px-Island_Green_Shopping_Centre%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_605649.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Island_Green_Shopping_Centre%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_605649.jpg/500px-Island_Green_Shopping_Centre%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_605649.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The retail park and its car park","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIsland_Green_Shopping_Centre%2C_Wrexham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_605649.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Path_to_Wrexham_Central_railway_station_%28geograph_5672770%29.jpg/500px-Path_to_Wrexham_Central_railway_station_%28geograph_5672770%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Path_to_Wrexham_Central_railway_station_%28geograph_5672770%29.jpg/500px-Path_to_Wrexham_Central_railway_station_%28geograph_5672770%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pathway to 1999 iteration of Wrexham Central railway station, within the retail park, situated between two retail stores","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APath_to_Wrexham_Central_railway_station_(geograph_5672770).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Salzproduktion-Halle.jpg/500px-Salzproduktion-Halle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://th